WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.110 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  in the world's most powerful country a  sharp turn away from right-wing populism  the inauguration of democrat joe biden  donald trump became the first outgoing  president in modern u.s history not to  attend  but two weeks before the inauguration  many of trump's loyal supporters  attacked the united states capitol  building in washington following an  unsuccessful bid by trump to overturn  his defeat  today  we celebrate the triumph not of a  candidate but of a cause  the cause of democracy  the people  the will of the people has been heard  and the will of the people has been  heeded  for the first time a biracial woman  kamel harris was elected as the u.s vice  president ushering in what many see as a  milestone  across the atlantic the end of an era  after 16 years of leading her country  and europe german chancellor angela  merkel decided that it was time to step  down  after general elections she was replaced  earlier this month by ola schultz a  social democrat  this coming year could see more changes  in europe  incumbent french president emmanuel  macron is expected to face strong  competition in april  the leader of the right-wing national  rally party marine le pen has launched  her third bid for the presidency she too  faces stiff competition from a far right  candidate eric zemer who has been  compared to donald trump  in hungary right-wing nationalist victor  orban has held office as prime minister  for the last 11 years and in april he  too will face a strong electoral  challenge  2021 was marked by an alarming number of  coup d'etats and growing  authoritarianism  there were coups in mali and guinea a  military takeover in sudan an  unsuccessful coup attempt in niger and  an arbitrary transfer of power in chad  in the new year kenya and angola will  hold general elections and mali will see  if the transitional authorities do in  fact hold elections as promised  in the middle east syria's president  bashar al-assad obtained 95 of the vote  in a re-election that was widely  described as fraudulent  while in iran ultra hardliner ibrahim  ricey was declared the winner in an  election that saw the lowest turnout in  iranian history  in latin america 2021 was a turbulent  year with nicaragua's leader daniel  ortega assuring his re-election last  month after disqualifying and arresting  the vast majority of his would-be  competitors  but in neighboring honduras cio mata  castro the wife of a former president  who was ousted in 2009 was elected as  the country's first woman president  [Music]  the year ended with a 35-year-old former  left-wing student leader gabriel borrice  elected in chile with the largest number  of votes in history  there's no indication that the region's  more authoritarian governments will be  less so in the new year  but after a decade-long swing to the  right the election of gabriel borrice in  chile could signal the beginning of a  new shift back to the left in the  continent starting with latin america's  largest country brazil  the new year will pit populist  conservative president jayed bolsonaro  against his arch political foe luis  inacio lula da silva brazil's left-wing  former president whose prison conviction  for corruption was overturned in april  and was now leading in the polls  but they will also compete against the  man who sent lula to prison former  anti-corruption czar sergio moro  colombia could also shift from the right  to the left in may polls put former  bogota mayor gustavo petro in the lead  south korea will hold presidential  elections in the new year  but perhaps the most attention will be  on the philippines where current  president rodrigo duterte could be  replaced by ferdinand marcos jr the son  of the country's former dictator and  duterte's daughter sarah who's running  as his vice president there's concern  that the ticket aims to perpetuate their  respective political dynasties  lucia newman al jazeera santiago

